WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:04.990
Film Courage: Jeffrey, going back to before
you were a Hollywood screenwriter, what made

00:00:04.990 --> 00:00:08.170
you think you could tell stories on a Hollywood
level?

00:00:08.170 --> 00:00:12.610
What gave you the confidence or that feeling
(intuition) that “You know what?

00:00:12.610 --> 00:00:13.910
This is something I think I can do.”

00:00:13.910 --> 00:00:16.650
Final Destination Franchise Screenwriter Jeffrey
Reddick: I think it’s the ignorance of the

00:00:16.650 --> 00:00:17.650
young!

00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:22.369
[Laughs] I talked to a friend of mine once
(probably a couple of years ago when I was

00:00:22.369 --> 00:00:25.860
home [in Kentucky] and when I was 8 or 9 she
asked me what I wanted to do when I grew up

00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:27.050
and I said I wanted to be a movie star.

00:00:27.050 --> 00:00:30.570
So I kind of always knew I wanted to work
in film.

00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:33.710
Growing up I thought I wanted to be an actor.

00:00:33.710 --> 00:00:37.269
And I went to New York to study acting actually
at the beginning of my career and that didn’t

00:00:37.269 --> 00:00:41.030
pan out really because non-traditional casting
wasn't a thing back in the early 90’s.

00:00:41.030 --> 00:00:44.850
And they cancelled The Cosby Show show so
without non-traditional casting, that was

00:00:44.850 --> 00:00:47.679
your only place.

00:00:47.679 --> 00:00:52.339
English was my second favorite subject and
I’d been writing from the time I was young.

00:00:52.339 --> 00:00:56.319
I would always write stories and my English
teachers were kind of my biggest inspirations

00:00:56.319 --> 00:00:57.319
as far as school goes.

00:00:57.319 --> 00:01:00.129
So I was like “Well, I’ll just write stuff
to be in.”

00:01:00.129 --> 00:01:02.920
That’s kind of why I segued into writing.

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:07.670
When you’re younger, you just don’t think
of…Hollywood does seem like this faraway

00:01:07.670 --> 00:01:11.969
magical place in a way but you are also very
naive about how the movie business is so you

00:01:11.969 --> 00:01:16.310
think you just write something and somebody
likes it and they go out and pick up a camera

00:01:16.310 --> 00:01:18.810
and they make it and then it comes out in
the theater.

00:01:18.810 --> 00:01:20.659
When you’re younger that’s all you can
think about.

00:01:20.659 --> 00:01:26.390
You don’t know all of the convoluted, twisted
process of screenwriting, getting something

00:01:26.390 --> 00:01:28.070
from page to screen when you’re young.

00:01:28.070 --> 00:01:33.590
You’re in that magical, illusion land where
you’re like “I can do it!”

00:01:33.590 --> 00:01:41.079
Again, writing wasn’t always my first goal,
but working in movies has always pretty much

00:01:41.079 --> 00:01:43.039
been just what I’ve wanted to do my whole
life.

00:01:43.039 --> 00:01:46.400
That was kind of my only career game plan.

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:50.009
Film Courage: How small was the town that
you grew up in?

00:01:50.009 --> 00:01:53.670
Jeffrey Reddick: I think we had 100 students
in our graduating class.

00:01:53.670 --> 00:01:56.390
I almost feel like I should Google that (I
should know that).

00:01:56.390 --> 00:01:57.420
It was a very small town.

00:01:57.420 --> 00:02:01.880
It took us about a 40-minute bus ride to get
to school every day.

00:02:01.880 --> 00:02:05.229
We were the last stop out there on the hill.

00:02:05.229 --> 00:02:07.039
It was in Eastern Kentucky.

00:02:07.039 --> 00:02:08.590
It was a very small town.

00:02:08.590 --> 00:02:09.910
They didn’t have a theater program.

00:02:09.910 --> 00:02:13.380
My English teacher (when I was a Sophomore),
I talked her into creating a theater program

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:20.350
for the one year and then it died out after
that year.

00:02:20.350 --> 00:02:22.880
But I was pretty determined.

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:25.990
It’s very interesting when you’re young
and you’re driven.

00:02:25.990 --> 00:02:31.090
That’s one of the things you try to tell
people to hold onto because as you get a little

00:02:31.090 --> 00:02:35.120
older, the drive doesn’t necessarily go
away but the realities of the business and

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:36.900
the distractions can get in your way.

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:44.170
When you’re young you just have that boundless
energy and faith and optimism and you try

00:02:44.170 --> 00:02:45.380
to hold onto that magic.

00:02:45.380 --> 00:02:48.480
You know even when you start getting into
the business side of things.

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:51.550
Film Courage: Right…because as you get older
you kind of talk yourself out of it knowing

00:02:51.550 --> 00:02:55.910
all of the things that can go wrong and it’s
almost a shame.

00:02:55.910 --> 00:03:00.930
There is that protective part of someone which
is there for good reason but it talks you

00:03:00.930 --> 00:03:01.930
out of it.

00:03:01.930 --> 00:03:05.250
Jeffrey Reddick: Yeah…and I think nobody
encourages a career in the arts.

00:03:05.250 --> 00:03:08.080
If you tell anybody when you’re young that
you want to be an actor or a writer, they

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:09.740
are always saying “Yeah…that’s great.

00:03:09.740 --> 00:03:11.580
What are you going to do for a real job?”

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:13.000
That’s pretty much the reaction.

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:17.540
My Mom did not know that I majored in theater
in college until after I was in college.

00:03:17.540 --> 00:03:23.170
I’d tell her I was majoring in math and
she got so mad when you found out I majored

00:03:23.170 --> 00:03:25.530
in theater because she was like “You are
never going to be able to make a living doing

00:03:25.530 --> 00:03:29.070
that.”

00:03:29.070 --> 00:03:35.410
So there is the reality, but for me I just
never had a back-up plan.

00:03:35.410 --> 00:03:39.190
Luckily I was good at English and writing
was another passion of mine so I found a way

00:03:39.190 --> 00:03:44.320
to stay in the business and not give up when
the acting didn’t pan out as soon as I had

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:47.020
hoped it would.

00:03:47.020 --> 00:03:50.130
People will usually tell anybody who says
they want to be in the film business or any

00:03:50.130 --> 00:03:52.150
kind of art “Oh that will be a good hobby.

00:03:52.150 --> 00:03:54.330
What are you going to do for a real job?”

00:03:54.330 --> 00:03:55.540
That’s just part of our society.

00:03:55.540 --> 00:03:58.640
We don’t really value it in this country.

00:03:58.640 --> 00:04:01.670
In other countries they do value their arts
more than we do here.

00:04:01.670 --> 00:04:05.040
Usually arts programs are the first things
to get cut in schools.

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:10.010
It’s like sports number one, then science
and math and stuff.

00:04:10.010 --> 00:04:15.150
The arts are usually down here as far as priorities
go which is interesting because I think the

00:04:15.150 --> 00:04:20.130
arts are probably one of the more helpful,
just to help you as a human being to grow

00:04:20.130 --> 00:04:25.950
and expand your horizons, open your mind and
get in touch with your emotions.

00:04:25.950 --> 00:04:31.380
A lot of the other fields don’t really emphasize
or help you do that.

00:04:31.380 --> 00:04:34.210
That’s why a lot of people love the arts.

00:04:34.210 --> 00:04:39.420
That’s something we have to start valuing
a lot more in our country.

00:04:39.420 --> 00:04:42.890
Film Courage: It’s funny you mention sports
because I think I heard you say in another

00:04:42.890 --> 00:04:47.820
interview (and I laughed out loud when I heard
it) that people who are into horror probably

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:51.400
weren’t people who were playing sports [Laughs].

00:04:51.400 --> 00:04:54.190
They weren’t the cheerleaders and they weren’t
the jocks.

00:04:54.190 --> 00:04:55.320
I thought that was great.

00:04:55.320 --> 00:04:59.810
It’s true…you develop this interesting
imagination that when you’re in this sort

00:04:59.810 --> 00:05:05.000
of this herd mentality trying to be popular,
all that [interesting ideas for material]

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:06.490
isn’t probably going through your mind.

00:05:06.490 --> 00:05:07.770
Maybe you can talk about that?

00:05:07.770 --> 00:05:12.310
Jeffrey Reddick: There’s a lot too I mean
if you’re an athlete, that’s your whole

00:05:12.310 --> 00:05:13.310
life.

00:05:13.310 --> 00:05:14.310
Like you’re always practicing.

00:05:14.310 --> 00:05:17.600
When you’re not at school practicing, you’re
practicing at home, you’re watching what

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:19.220
you eat, you’re working out.

00:05:19.220 --> 00:05:21.370
That’s your whole life.

00:05:21.370 --> 00:05:25.060
I think most of the people who I know that
are artists, they spend most of their time

00:05:25.060 --> 00:05:32.740
at home watching movies or reading comic books
or doing kind of nerdy, geeky things, really

00:05:32.740 --> 00:05:34.960
playing in their imagination or with their
imagination.

00:05:34.960 --> 00:05:39.670
I think that’s a common thing you’ll find
with artists.

00:05:39.670 --> 00:05:45.960
A lot of artists didn’t have a big clique
that they hung out with or weren’t really,

00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:46.960
super popular.

00:05:46.960 --> 00:05:48.400
Some of them were.

00:05:48.400 --> 00:05:53.400
Especially in High School, school is like
a machine.

00:05:53.400 --> 00:05:55.290
It’s very important obviously to get an
education.

00:05:55.290 --> 00:05:59.770
When you have so many kids every year that
you don’t have time…everybody’s got

00:05:59.770 --> 00:06:03.010
their own individual personalities and you
can’t really get to know people so you have

00:06:03.010 --> 00:06:07.690
to shove them in boxes just to process people.

00:06:07.690 --> 00:06:10.210
It’s a weird thing that I noticed as we
grow up.

00:06:10.210 --> 00:06:13.870
As people still try to generalize in a way.

00:06:13.870 --> 00:06:18.060
That’s even when I talk about jocks and
the cheerleaders aren’t the ones that were

00:06:18.060 --> 00:06:22.060
at home watching a lot of horror movies, it’s
also because they were out practicing and

00:06:22.060 --> 00:06:25.110
doing their thing.

00:06:25.110 --> 00:06:29.200
But it’s interesting how we still keep those
boxes as we get older…you know…I sound

00:06:29.200 --> 00:06:33.320
a little bit like a hippy right now.

00:06:33.320 --> 00:06:37.389
Film Courage: I found it funny because there
is a lot of truth to it.

